WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:03.169 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:05.071
Hey, everybody. I'm just gonna
get started right away.

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:08.374
Japan does have lawns.
So here's the thing.

00:00:08.374 --> 00:00:10.309
[ Laughter ]

00:00:10.309 --> 00:00:13.046
I don't even remember the joke,
but all of a sudden,

00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:16.749
a lot of commenters are telling
me, "Japan doesn't have lawns."

00:00:16.749 --> 00:00:18.918
So I come out here,
I correct myself,

00:00:18.918 --> 00:00:20.887
and now everybody's saying,
"Dude, you're crazy.

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:22.288
Japan does have lawns."

00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:25.591
Look. You guys figure it out.
I've never been there.

00:00:25.591 --> 00:00:27.527
I want to go.
It's on my bucket list.

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:30.063
But this is the last
I'm gonna say about it.

00:00:32.898 --> 00:00:35.068
We talked once again

00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:39.605
about the difference
in the spelling of "psych."

00:00:39.605 --> 00:00:42.508
When you say to someone,
"Psych!

00:00:42.508 --> 00:00:46.078
is it spelled P-S-Y-C-H

00:00:46.078 --> 00:00:49.348
or is it spelled S-I-K-E?

00:00:49.348 --> 00:00:52.718
Which is how we spelled it
a couple weeks ago

00:00:52.718 --> 00:00:56.155
at this point in the broadcast.

00:00:56.155 --> 00:00:58.891
People feel very strongly
that the correct spelling

00:00:58.891 --> 00:01:03.062
is P-S-Y-C-H and not S-I-K-E.

00:01:03.062 --> 00:01:04.663
Also, I didn't know this.

00:01:04.663 --> 00:01:08.735
"Sike" is a small stream.

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:12.572
You guys want to see a picture
of a small stream?

00:01:14.307 --> 00:01:15.842
[ Laughter ]

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:20.413
Psych.

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:22.148
[ Laughter ]

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:33.626
Um, I said that Biden
tried to talk to a turkey

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:35.628
when he was pardoning
the turkeys.

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:38.431
A bunch of you said,
"No. He did."

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:41.067
[ Laughter ]

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:43.503
He maybe tried to have
a conversation,

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:46.872
but he definitely
talked to the turkey.

00:01:46.872 --> 00:01:48.674
Apologies.

00:01:48.674 --> 00:01:52.478
Also, someone said, "Fauci
is two years older than Biden.

00:01:52.478 --> 00:01:55.481
Why don't you make fun of him
for being old?"

00:01:55.481 --> 00:01:56.816
It's really just, I guess,

00:01:56.816 --> 00:02:00.286
the fact that Fauci never
kind of just wandered off.

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:04.156
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.156 --> 00:02:06.859
Fauci looks great for his age.

00:02:06.859 --> 00:02:12.398
And remember -- it's easy to
forget he had to stand on stage

00:02:12.398 --> 00:02:16.201
when Donald Trump
tried to do science.

00:02:16.201 --> 00:02:19.005
That'll age a person.

00:02:19.005 --> 00:02:20.673
Hey, another thing I just
want to say in general

00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:22.809
as I look back on the pandemic.

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:25.244
Look, did everybody get it right
from the beginning?

00:02:25.244 --> 00:02:27.880
Did Fauci make zero mistakes?

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.682
Absolutely not.
It's, you know, a pandemic.

00:02:30.682 --> 00:02:32.585
You're having to make decisions
quick in real time.

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:35.387
I think, overall, job well done.

00:02:35.387 --> 00:02:36.889
But this is something
I really want to hammer home.

00:02:36.889 --> 00:02:39.258
Just because a doctor
makes a mistake

00:02:39.258 --> 00:02:42.095
doesn't mean
you're the doctor now.

00:02:46.532 --> 00:02:47.900
Oh, so, this is interesting.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:52.304
We did a "Closer Look"
where we talked about

00:02:52.304 --> 00:02:55.307
MyPillow
and Mike Lindell's pillow,

00:02:55.307 --> 00:02:59.445
and there was a sound effect
that played

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:04.250
when I dropped the pillow
that was very late.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.252
And I said,
"That's the problem of

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:10.656
having a sound guy
who's in Australia."

00:03:10.656 --> 00:03:14.427
But then a bunch of you pointed
out Australia is ahead of us.

00:03:14.427 --> 00:03:18.932
So if he was there,
the sound effect would be early.

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:20.866
So it would have been like this.

00:03:20.866 --> 00:03:22.202
[ Clang! ]

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:27.640
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.411 --> 00:03:34.480
Alright.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.650
We talked about Johnny Bananas.
We didn't overdo it.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.352
[ Laughter ]

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:44.090
We talked about how
Johnny Bananas' friends

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:45.825
would often play a mean trick

00:03:45.825 --> 00:03:47.760
where they'd set him up
on a blind date.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.496
He'd go to a restaurant.
There'd be a --

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:51.897
There'd be a monkey in lipstick.

00:03:51.897 --> 00:03:53.766
Well, we showed a picture.

00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:55.334
Once again --
And this is the problem

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:58.804
we've been getting in --
year-long problem --

00:03:58.804 --> 00:04:01.807
which is we showed
a chimpanzee in lipstick,

00:04:01.807 --> 00:04:04.877
which is an ape, not a monkey.

00:04:04.877 --> 00:04:08.180
We took the photo with a monkey

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:10.549
and then after the fact

00:04:10.549 --> 00:04:13.019
found out the monkey
was nonunion.

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:16.255
[ Laughter ]

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:19.592
And in the 11th hour,

00:04:19.592 --> 00:04:23.062
the best we could do
was a chimpanzee.

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:28.534
Kenny was the one
who knew the monkey...

00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:30.870
Remember? He was like,
"I've seen that monkey before.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.506
Crossing.
Crossed the picket line."

00:04:33.506 --> 00:04:35.508
[ Laughter ]

00:04:39.578 --> 00:04:42.214
Difference --
monkeys have tails.

00:04:42.214 --> 00:04:43.616
That's how you know
the difference

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:45.784
between an ape and a monkey.

00:04:45.784 --> 00:04:47.453
And this is a story --
I did not hear it.

00:04:47.453 --> 00:04:49.021
I am not friends
with any chimpanzees.

00:04:49.021 --> 00:04:50.890
I have a friend who's friends
with chimpanzees.

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:52.458
He told me this story
about this chimpanzee

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:54.193
who's on a date
with another chimpanzee.

00:04:54.193 --> 00:04:55.762
And I guess
it's going great, right?

00:04:55.762 --> 00:04:57.730
And they go back
to the chimpanzee's apartment,

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:00.833
and the other chimpanzee
goes into the bathroom,

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:02.601
and the other chimpanzee
is thinking,

00:05:02.601 --> 00:05:04.070
"I'm gonna get it on."

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:08.574
And then the other chimpanzee
comes out, takes off the robe,

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:10.910
and the first chimpanzee
sees the tail.

00:05:12.378 --> 00:05:14.080
And that's all you need
to know about...

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:16.749
-♪ About the crying game ♪

00:05:16.749 --> 00:05:18.351
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.253 --> 00:05:22.555
[ Clang! ]

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:38.103
I was told -- I did this
for "six," and someone said,

00:05:38.103 --> 00:05:40.973
"Don't do -- Don't --
That's bad."

00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:43.209
I don't know
the right way to do "six."

00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:48.114
But there was -- I guess --

00:05:48.114 --> 00:05:53.819
I've seen deleted scenes from
"Inglourious Basterds."

00:05:53.819 --> 00:05:56.789
You know, 'cause there's
that giveaway scene?

00:05:56.789 --> 00:05:58.390
I guess he goes, "Drei Glaesen."

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:00.660
And that's the giveaway
that he's not German.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:04.030
And I guess in the original
it's, "Drei Glaesen.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.131
And sechs jalapeño poppers."

00:06:06.131 --> 00:06:08.134
[ Laughs ]

00:06:08.134 --> 00:06:10.169
[ Laughter ]

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:13.906
I don't know, guys.

00:06:15.908 --> 00:06:20.379
We talked about
President Samberg.

00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:22.014
How Obama's a good comedian

00:06:22.014 --> 00:06:24.016
but none of the comedians
I know would be good presidents.

00:06:24.016 --> 00:06:25.918
And we just showed a picture
of President Samberg,

00:06:25.918 --> 00:06:27.420
but he was a little
too neat-looking.

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:28.921
It was like modern Samberg.

00:06:28.921 --> 00:06:33.626
I wanted it to look a little bit
more like the Doofy Samberg.

00:06:33.626 --> 00:06:35.528
And then we did a lot
of Samberg material.

00:06:35.528 --> 00:06:40.332
And I'm, you know --
I bet Andy saw it, right?

00:06:40.332 --> 00:06:44.136
I mean, we talked about him
on TV in a "Closer Look."

00:06:44.136 --> 00:06:45.704
And if he --
You know, if not him,

00:06:45.704 --> 00:06:47.606
certainly you would think
one of his friends would say,

00:06:47.606 --> 00:06:49.842
"Hey, I saw you on..."

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:52.611
Let's find out.
[ Voice call ringing ]

00:06:52.611 --> 00:06:54.514
Been a while
since we've called Andy.

00:06:56.382 --> 00:06:57.683
[ Bee-oop ]

00:06:57.683 --> 00:06:59.485
Nope.

00:06:59.485 --> 00:07:02.121
Straight to unavailable.

00:07:02.121 --> 00:07:05.958
Guess he's doing a spelling bee.

00:07:05.958 --> 00:07:10.195
We talked about Flat Earthers
and are there Flat Mooners?

00:07:10.195 --> 00:07:11.997
And from what I can tell,

00:07:11.997 --> 00:07:14.901
no, there's not
a Flat Mooners Society.

00:07:14.901 --> 00:07:17.303
So I organized
and we had our first meeting

00:07:17.303 --> 00:07:19.305
because I like the idea

00:07:19.305 --> 00:07:21.373
that one of the celestial bodies
would be flat.

00:07:21.373 --> 00:07:23.175
I think that's a fun thing
to sit around

00:07:23.175 --> 00:07:25.844
with like-minded people
and talk about, right?

00:07:25.844 --> 00:07:27.913
Everybody likes the idea
that they live somewhere

00:07:27.913 --> 00:07:31.283
where they could walk to a point
and then be like, "Whoa!"

00:07:31.283 --> 00:07:33.586
I mean, who wouldn't want that?

00:07:33.586 --> 00:07:37.723
Anyway -- And I think Flat
Mooning is off to a great start.

00:07:37.723 --> 00:07:41.627
I guess
some Flat Earthers think

00:07:41.627 --> 00:07:45.097
the moon is a projection
onto a dome.

00:07:45.097 --> 00:07:47.033
Which would work...

00:07:49.302 --> 00:07:51.103
But Flat Mooners,
we're off to a great start.

00:07:51.103 --> 00:07:53.005
We do hope
to increase our ranks.

00:07:53.005 --> 00:07:55.707
And one of the nice things
about Flat Mooners is --

00:07:55.707 --> 00:07:58.277
and one of the hard, fast rules

00:07:58.277 --> 00:08:01.414
is we're not gonna let it be
a gateway to anti-Semitism!

00:08:01.414 --> 00:08:03.749
[ Laughter ]

00:08:03.749 --> 00:08:05.451
We're gonna get together.
We're gonna talk about

00:08:05.451 --> 00:08:08.454
how the moon's flat
and nothing else!

00:08:08.454 --> 00:08:10.088
Okay?

00:08:10.088 --> 00:08:11.957
If somebody
all of a sudden says,

00:08:11.957 --> 00:08:13.959
"Hey, you know, if you believe
that, I got something..."

00:08:13.959 --> 00:08:16.795
We're gonna be like, "No!

00:08:16.795 --> 00:08:18.898
Don't you have a game tonight?!

00:08:18.898 --> 00:08:20.733
[ Laughter ]

00:08:22.034 --> 00:08:24.903
Um, also a lot of people --

00:08:24.903 --> 00:08:26.939
Because I said the moon's flat.

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:31.877
They made very juvenile jokes
about how I have a flat ass.

00:08:31.877 --> 00:08:34.880
No, sir.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:37.617
But that did -- You know,
I did want to have

00:08:37.617 --> 00:08:40.986
a little bit of body positivity
after I engaged with that.

00:08:40.986 --> 00:08:43.789
And you guys remember
when I didn't --

00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:45.691
when I wore my tank top.

00:08:45.691 --> 00:08:48.260
[ Laughter ]

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:50.462
Some people said, "Oh, that --

00:08:50.462 --> 00:08:52.631
I guess that proves Seth
doesn't have any tattoos."

00:08:52.631 --> 00:08:54.433
I actually do, but if I know --

00:08:54.433 --> 00:08:57.203
And this happens
to a lot of people in showbiz

00:08:57.203 --> 00:08:59.105
who get tattoos --
you have to have them covered

00:08:59.105 --> 00:09:01.106
with makeup
before you go on stage.

00:09:01.106 --> 00:09:04.109
But here's --
If you want to know, I think,

00:09:04.109 --> 00:09:06.311
amongst the Jackals,
I'm happy to share.

00:09:06.311 --> 00:09:08.814
I do have one tattoo.

00:09:08.814 --> 00:09:10.082
There you go.

00:09:15.955 --> 00:09:21.727
Hey. Rhaenyra's dragon
is a female dragon. Syrax.

00:09:21.727 --> 00:09:24.329
We did say Trump
thought it was a he.

00:09:24.329 --> 00:09:28.433
I do think Trump would think
all dragons are boys.

00:09:28.433 --> 00:09:30.236
I'm not saying that's --
[ Clang! ]

00:09:35.341 --> 00:09:39.412
The worst part is --
in order -- I can't --

00:09:39.412 --> 00:09:42.715
It's so hard to tell them
to stop doing it.

00:09:42.715 --> 00:09:45.083
[ Laughter ]

00:09:45.083 --> 00:09:47.886
To get them to stop now,

00:09:47.886 --> 00:09:50.389
I would have had to
call them yesterday.

00:09:58.864 --> 00:10:01.333
By the way, someone wrote,
"There are so many awkward

00:10:01.333 --> 00:10:03.002
pauses filled
with fake laughter.

00:10:03.002 --> 00:10:06.071
It just becomes off-putting.
This show is so hit-or-miss."

00:10:06.071 --> 00:10:07.640
Dude...

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:09.841
[ Laughter ]

00:10:09.841 --> 00:10:12.444
That was -- Before we decided
on the name "Corrections,"

00:10:12.444 --> 00:10:14.614
we were going to call this
"Hit or Miss."

00:10:18.083 --> 00:10:21.119
We said Santa Claus
was 2,000 years old.

00:10:21.119 --> 00:10:24.457
And a bunch of you who...

00:10:24.457 --> 00:10:27.526
I guess think he's a real...

00:10:27.526 --> 00:10:30.429
[ Laughter ]

00:10:30.429 --> 00:10:32.498
They're like, "No,
he's based on Saint Nick,

00:10:32.498 --> 00:10:36.101
who was, like, born in 280 A.D."

00:10:36.101 --> 00:10:38.503
And also somebody said,
"If he's 2,000,

00:10:38.503 --> 00:10:41.240
the picture you use
at most looks 100."

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:43.075
So we did -- we went
to our graphics department,

00:10:43.075 --> 00:10:45.444
who worked really hard.
We're like, "What do you think

00:10:45.444 --> 00:10:47.847
a 2,000-year-old Santa
would look like?"

00:10:47.847 --> 00:10:50.149
And I think that's...
I think that's...

00:10:50.149 --> 00:10:52.184
[ Laughter ]

00:10:57.022 --> 00:11:00.225
It's exciting. Holiday movies.

00:11:00.225 --> 00:11:03.161
You know, we don't --
our kids don't watch a bunch --

00:11:03.161 --> 00:11:05.130
You know, we don't have
a lot of screen time.

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:06.765
But when we travel
for the holidays,

00:11:06.765 --> 00:11:09.301
when we're on the plane
going to see their grandparents,

00:11:09.301 --> 00:11:12.938
we will let them watch
a holiday film.

00:11:12.938 --> 00:11:14.573
And so I've been spending
a lot of time

00:11:14.573 --> 00:11:16.241
trying to pick
the perfect holiday film,

00:11:16.241 --> 00:11:17.743
and I'm hoping it goes better

00:11:17.743 --> 00:11:20.980
than last year
when I flew with my boys.

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:24.416
We decided to watch
"A Charlie Brown Christmas,"

00:11:24.416 --> 00:11:27.719
which is a classic film,
endures over the years.

00:11:27.719 --> 00:11:32.391
I downloaded it on my iPad.
Sitting with my two sons.

00:11:32.391 --> 00:11:34.859
I've got one of them
on either side of me,

00:11:34.859 --> 00:11:36.962
set up the iPad
once we take off.

00:11:36.962 --> 00:11:39.098
I waited until we took off.

00:11:39.098 --> 00:11:41.567
I did not have
my electronic device off --

00:11:41.567 --> 00:11:43.068
on, excuse me -- during takeoff.

00:11:43.068 --> 00:11:44.836
Because I did that once,
and the plane crashed.

00:11:44.836 --> 00:11:46.839
So I didn't...

00:11:46.839 --> 00:11:49.141
I'm not gonna make
that mistake again.

00:11:49.141 --> 00:11:51.376
[ Laughter ]

00:11:51.376 --> 00:11:53.446
So, I have my iPad out,
you know,

00:11:53.446 --> 00:11:57.215
on its little stand, and --

00:11:57.215 --> 00:12:00.519
I was just --
I don't even know if my --

00:12:00.519 --> 00:12:04.190
I'm worried my kids are not
gonna want to watch anything

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:06.625
on a plane again
because the minute it started,

00:12:06.625 --> 00:12:10.029
the stewardess ran.

00:12:10.029 --> 00:12:16.068
Ran down the aisle and slammed
my iPad face-down.

00:12:16.068 --> 00:12:18.637
And we were all like...

00:12:18.637 --> 00:12:20.573
And I guess there was
someone on the flight

00:12:20.573 --> 00:12:22.341
who was allergic to peanuts.

00:12:22.341 --> 00:12:25.177
[ Laughter ]

00:12:25.177 --> 00:12:26.612
[ Laughs ]

00:12:40.092 --> 00:12:42.928
I asked Shoemaker --

00:12:42.928 --> 00:12:45.331
I asked Shoemaker what his
favorite Christmas movie was,

00:12:45.331 --> 00:12:48.634
and he goes, "'The Grinch,'
before he switched!"

00:12:48.634 --> 00:12:50.469
[ Laughter ]

00:12:53.439 --> 00:12:54.907
Don't get him started
on The Grinch.

00:12:54.907 --> 00:12:58.777
He's like, "He didn't have
the guts to finish the job!

00:12:58.777 --> 00:13:00.646
He blinked!"

00:13:06.285 --> 00:13:08.620
Someone wrote, "I want to meet
the graphics department

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:10.989
to see if they're the way
Seth makes them out to be."

00:13:10.989 --> 00:13:12.591
Well, they want
to meet you, too,

00:13:12.591 --> 00:13:16.428
and they're down for everything.

00:13:16.428 --> 00:13:18.630
I looked at one of their
Tinder profiles recently,

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:22.034
and the only thing it had on it
was "Turn-offs: none."

00:13:24.235 --> 00:13:25.471
This breaks my heart.

00:13:25.471 --> 00:13:27.005
My mom was here
the last time we did this,

00:13:27.005 --> 00:13:30.408
and I was talking about
the French Revolution.

00:13:30.408 --> 00:13:34.446
And I said, you know, people --
les personnes.

00:13:34.446 --> 00:13:37.082
French for "people."

00:13:37.082 --> 00:13:39.285
And everyone on YouTube
is saying,

00:13:39.285 --> 00:13:42.120
"You actually said 'poissons.'
Fish.

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:43.722
Which I'm like -- No, I didn't.

00:13:43.722 --> 00:13:47.560
I know what I said. I know
poissons. I know personnes.

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:50.229
And then someone basically says,

00:13:50.229 --> 00:13:54.200
"And I know you did because
YouTube agrees with me."

00:13:55.333 --> 00:13:58.070 line:0%
They -- You lower-thirded me
"poissons."

00:13:59.405 --> 00:14:03.008 line:0%
Am I not making enough money
for you, YouTube?

00:14:06.945 --> 00:14:08.848
I'm genuinely curious.

00:14:14.653 --> 00:14:16.555
Allergic to peanuts.

00:14:16.555 --> 00:14:18.624
[ Laughter ]

00:14:21.326 --> 00:14:23.328
Hey, um, you know,
I originally said --

00:14:23.328 --> 00:14:25.096
You know, we were talking
about the French Revolution,

00:14:25.096 --> 00:14:26.265
and I said 19th century,

00:14:26.265 --> 00:14:28.033
and everybody's like,
"No. 18th century."

00:14:28.033 --> 00:14:29.268
But, of course, in "Les Mis,"

00:14:29.268 --> 00:14:30.935
which is what
we were talking about,

00:14:30.935 --> 00:14:33.171
it was the 19th century,
because what they do in France,

00:14:33.171 --> 00:14:35.307
they just keep having them.
There's a lot of them.

00:14:35.307 --> 00:14:39.478
And there were uprisings
in 1830, 1832, 1848.

00:14:39.478 --> 00:14:41.346
I was referring --

00:14:41.346 --> 00:14:44.516
"Les Mis" is about
the July revolution in 1830,

00:14:44.516 --> 00:14:47.119
and that was the one
where I was talking about

00:14:47.119 --> 00:14:49.054
how they stacked up
all the furniture,

00:14:49.054 --> 00:14:51.656
and that was the most --

00:14:51.656 --> 00:14:53.525
I was making the argument
that was the most furniture

00:14:53.525 --> 00:14:56.161
that was ever stacked
in an armed conflict.

00:14:56.161 --> 00:14:57.462
But I was corrected.

00:14:57.462 --> 00:15:00.733
The most furniture stacked
in an armed conflict...

00:15:02.701 --> 00:15:05.270
Never mind. I feel like --

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:10.708
I feel like this crowd today
isn't reacting to the jokes

00:15:10.708 --> 00:15:12.411
the way they deserve.

00:15:12.411 --> 00:15:13.979
[ Laughter ]

00:15:19.484 --> 00:15:21.320
The war that had
the most furniture stacking

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:23.389
took place
in the Ottoman Empire!

00:15:23.389 --> 00:15:25.591
[ Laughter ]

00:15:31.096 --> 00:15:34.065
Hey, we -- I kind of --
I threw a little shade

00:15:34.065 --> 00:15:35.667
to intermittent fasting
last week.

00:15:35.667 --> 00:15:37.202
A lot of you said

00:15:37.202 --> 00:15:39.838
intermittent fasting
has done wonders for you

00:15:39.838 --> 00:15:41.573
and has helped
your health problems.

00:15:41.573 --> 00:15:43.675
And so I want to apologize
for saying

00:15:43.675 --> 00:15:46.878
that intermittent fasting
is a diet that doesn't work.

00:15:46.878 --> 00:15:49.615
I stand corrected, but maybe be
a bit more intermittent

00:15:49.615 --> 00:15:51.550
when you bring it up at parties.

00:15:54.653 --> 00:15:56.988
"Teen Wolf."

00:15:56.988 --> 00:16:01.293
We talked about Obama,
when he was seven,

00:16:01.293 --> 00:16:05.397
trying to decide if vampires
or werewolves were cool.

00:16:05.397 --> 00:16:08.634
And a lot of you said --

00:16:08.634 --> 00:16:11.836
He referenced "Teen Wolf"
in that argument.

00:16:11.836 --> 00:16:14.106
A lot of you said
when he was seven, it was 1968.

00:16:14.106 --> 00:16:16.174
"Teen Wolf" hadn't come out yet.

00:16:16.174 --> 00:16:18.109
But a lot of you don't know
"Teen Wolf" is not --

00:16:18.109 --> 00:16:19.578
is based on a real dude.

00:16:21.879 --> 00:16:23.582
Who was on the 1968 --

00:16:23.582 --> 00:16:27.419
Which -- Now the math works out
perfectly, Shoemaker.

00:16:27.419 --> 00:16:32.391
Was on the 1968 US Men's
Olympic basketball team.

00:16:33.959 --> 00:16:36.829
I think I know which one he is.
I'm trying to...

00:16:38.230 --> 00:16:41.467
There he is.

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:50.675
Hey, we had this art card --
Not art card. We had this art.

00:16:50.675 --> 00:16:51.609
And, um...

00:16:51.609 --> 00:16:53.878
At this point in the broadcast.

00:16:53.878 --> 00:16:55.614
And we said Waldo's in it.

00:16:55.614 --> 00:17:00.685
And we asked you to pause it
and look for stuff and...

00:17:00.685 --> 00:17:02.454
A bunch of you said
Waldo's not in it.

00:17:02.454 --> 00:17:04.556
Well, I will tell you now
that Waldo is in it.

00:17:04.556 --> 00:17:06.958
A very little bit of Waldo
is in it.

00:17:06.958 --> 00:17:09.928
And if you can find it...

00:17:09.928 --> 00:17:12.030
circle it...

00:17:12.030 --> 00:17:14.466
and mail it in to us.

00:17:14.466 --> 00:17:16.602
You get nothing!

00:17:16.602 --> 00:17:18.904 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:17:23.208 --> 00:17:27.012
I forgot --
This came in a while ago.

00:17:27.012 --> 00:17:30.549
Lovely letter
from a man named Phil.

00:17:30.549 --> 00:17:32.517
Um...

00:17:32.517 --> 00:17:35.687
He asked his son
to design a prototype

00:17:35.687 --> 00:17:37.856
for the Corrections mug
while we were waiting for ours.

00:17:37.856 --> 00:17:40.525
So this was
this summer actually.

00:17:40.525 --> 00:17:42.594
"My kid worked very hard

00:17:42.594 --> 00:17:44.696
and said that they drew
your face very accurately.

00:17:44.696 --> 00:17:46.799
I'll be happy to send people
at NBC the file with the design

00:17:46.799 --> 00:17:48.667
if you decide
to mass-produce this."

00:17:48.667 --> 00:17:51.370
Here is their Corrections mug.

00:17:52.804 --> 00:17:54.807
[ Laughter ]

00:17:58.243 --> 00:18:00.045
And we do appreciate
your son's hard work,

00:18:00.045 --> 00:18:01.646
and we're gonna
send him something --

00:18:01.646 --> 00:18:03.515
a cease-and-desist letter.

00:18:03.515 --> 00:18:05.350
[ Laughter ]

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:10.923
You guys.
I talked about the fact

00:18:10.923 --> 00:18:12.957
that I was going to --
I felt bad that I put us

00:18:12.957 --> 00:18:14.459
in a legal situation

00:18:14.459 --> 00:18:17.028
where all the foreign Jackals
who sent in postcards

00:18:17.028 --> 00:18:18.696
were then going to--

00:18:18.696 --> 00:18:21.132
Just gonna check real quick
if Samberg's said anything.

00:18:21.132 --> 00:18:23.135
And it's a hard "no."

00:18:23.135 --> 00:18:26.538
Um, let me see.
You guys aren't -- You --

00:18:26.538 --> 00:18:28.306
Anybody have anything to do?
Great.

00:18:28.306 --> 00:18:30.275
The thing --
I'm kind of dragging it out

00:18:30.275 --> 00:18:33.978
because we've got
our holiday party tonight.

00:18:33.978 --> 00:18:37.249
And I'm gonna try to keep the
staff here as long as possible,

00:18:37.249 --> 00:18:40.352
because I don't want them
to get too [bleep] up.

00:18:40.352 --> 00:18:42.254
[ Laughter ]

00:18:46.224 --> 00:18:51.129
So I said I didn't want NBC
to have to pay for the mugs.

00:18:51.129 --> 00:18:52.864
I'll pay for the mugs.

00:18:52.864 --> 00:18:55.700
Well, everybody was like,
"Oh, my God, Seth. So great."

00:18:55.700 --> 00:18:57.735
I do want to stress NBC said,

00:18:57.735 --> 00:18:59.737
"We're going to pay
for the mugs."

00:18:59.737 --> 00:19:02.140
But I felt bad
because it was my fault.

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:06.978
So I decided,
whatever the total was,

00:19:06.978 --> 00:19:09.581
I would take
that amount of money

00:19:09.581 --> 00:19:13.751
and I would give it
to a charity.

00:19:13.751 --> 00:19:15.653
That way,
NBC is paying for the mugs.

00:19:15.653 --> 00:19:18.089
I'm giving the money, the same
amount of money to the charity.

00:19:18.089 --> 00:19:19.825
So I looked around,
did some research,

00:19:19.825 --> 00:19:22.027
and I'm happy to announce
that I'm writing a check

00:19:22.027 --> 00:19:25.530
to an organization that I think
could really use the money.

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:27.032
NBC.

00:19:27.032 --> 00:19:29.401
[ Laughter ]

00:19:33.872 --> 00:19:35.607
You know what
the burden is there.

00:19:35.607 --> 00:19:37.643
They have a matching program.

00:19:37.643 --> 00:19:40.812
[ Laughter ]

00:19:40.812 --> 00:19:42.748
Well...

00:19:52.824 --> 00:19:54.926
[ Buzzes lips ]

00:19:54.926 --> 00:19:56.595
[ Laughter ]

00:20:01.466 --> 00:20:03.569
Still over an hour to the party.

00:20:07.205 --> 00:20:09.274
[Bleep]

00:20:13.378 --> 00:20:15.814
Aah!

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:20.852
AAH!

00:20:27.391 --> 00:20:29.661
I think I did it all.

00:20:36.367 --> 00:20:37.602
[ Clang! ]

00:20:37.602 --> 00:20:39.338
[ Laughter ]

00:20:47.345 --> 00:20:49.580
Go home!
Does anybody ever do that?

00:20:49.580 --> 00:20:51.483
Should that be my new sign-off?

00:20:53.085 --> 00:20:56.154
[ Laughter ]

00:20:56.154 --> 00:20:58.423
What did Tracey Ullman do?
Wasn't that it?

00:20:58.423 --> 00:20:59.992
"Go home"?

00:21:02.226 --> 00:21:04.362
[Bleep].
Still over an hour.

00:21:04.362 --> 00:21:06.431
[ Laughter ]

00:21:11.035 --> 00:21:12.404
See me next week.

